   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.5",
    "topic_title": "Membrane Transport",
    "stem": "A root cell contains an uncharged solute at 20 mM, while the soil water surrounding it contains the same solute at 2 mM. Measurements show that the solute continues to move into the cell through a membrane protein, so the inside concentration keeps rising. Which statement correctly describes this movement?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The solute moves from high to low concentration, so this is passive transport that requires no energy input.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the gradient, reading the higher inside value as the starting point even though the solute enters from the 2 mM side. Entering the cell means moving toward the 20 mM side, which is uphill. Fix: Identify where the solute starts and where it ends, then compare those two concentrations before naming the transport type."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The solute moves from low to high concentration, but this is passive transport because a membrane protein provides the pathway.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats protein involvement as proof of passive transport. Proteins can carry out either passive or active transport, and passive transport can only produce net movement from high to low concentration. Fix: Decide passive or active from the gradient direction and energy requirement, not from whether a protein is present."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The solute moves from high to low concentration, but this is active transport because any movement through a membrane protein requires ATP.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misreads the gradient and also assumes all protein-mediated transport uses ATP. Movement down a gradient through a protein needs no direct energy, and this solute is actually moving up its gradient. Fix: Ask whether net movement goes down or up the gradient first, and require energy only for uphill movement."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The solute moves from low to high concentration, so this is active transport that requires an energy input.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The solute enters from 2 mM outside to 20 mM inside, which is up its concentration gradient. Passive transport produces net movement only from high to low concentration. Net movement against the gradient requires energy, which defines active transport."
     }
    ]
   },
